Note on Visas & Scholarships for Ukrainians:

    EU: The EU activated the Temporary Protection Directive (TPD) for people fleeing Ukraine, granting rights like residence, access to education, and the labor market, initially for one year, potentially extendable up to March 2026.[14][15][16][17][18][19] This simplifies initial entry and stay. For long-term studies (over 3 months/1 year depending on specifics), you'll generally need a student visa and residence permit.[14][20][21] Specific scholarships for Ukrainians exist in Italy (e.g., funded by the Ministry of University and Research, regional bodies like ERDIS, or individual universities like Bologna, Padua, Turin) and the Netherlands (check university websites and general scholarship portals like Study in Holland).[22][23][24][25]

    UK: Ukrainian nationals arriving under specific schemes (like the Ukraine Family Scheme or Homes for Ukraine) are often eligible for 'home fee' status (lower tuition fees) and student finance (loans, grants).[12][26] Numerous UK universities offer scholarships and hardship funds for refugees and those affected by the war in Ukraine, including specific schemes for Ukrainians (e.g., University of Reading, Edinburgh, Warwick).[10][11][12][26] STAR (Student Action for Refugees) lists many relevant university scholarships.[10][12][26]

